    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d151">

                <pb facs="0479" n="1109" sameAs="frus:frus1873p1v2/pg1109"/>

                <head>No. 457.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. <persName corresp="#p_GHB1"
                            type="from">Boker</persName> to Mr. <persName corresp="#p_HF1" type="to"
                            >Fish</persName>.</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Constantinople</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1873-04-14"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 14, 1873</date>.
                        (Received May 3.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 93.]</seg>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to report that, owing to
                    the increasing scarcity of Arab horses in certain parts of the Ottoman empire,
                    the Sublime Porte, by a decree, communicated April 9, 1873, has forbidden the
                    exportation of horses of all breeds from the vilayets of Bagdad, of Syria, <pb
                        facs="0480" n="1110" xml:id="pg1110"/> and of Aleppo, for the next seven
                    years, dating from the day of the promulgation of the order.</p>
                <p>On account of the late bad harvests, and consequent suffering for want of food in
                    the Black Sea provinces of the empire, the Sublime Porte has issued a decree,
                    which was communicated April 12, 1873, interdicting the exportation of all kinds
                    of cereals from the districts of Roustchouk and of Vidin for the next three
                    months, beginning from the above date.</p>
                <p>As the above regulations may be of interest to some of the citizens of the United
                    States, the Sublime Porte requests that the decrees may be given publicity in
                    our country.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed><persName corresp="#p_GHB1">GEORGE H. BOKER</persName>.</signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
